1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:03,903
Filter design means juggling formulas:

2
00:00:04,003 --> 00:00:10,226
prototype elements, impedance matching,
microstrip dimensions, and the response.

3
00:00:10,326 --> 00:00:20,580
This calculator handles it all in one
page, for microstrip and discrete LC
filters, based on a 1990 thesis at TH Köln.

4
00:00:20,680 --> 00:00:23,900
Five pages, five views of the same design.

5
00:00:26,000 --> 00:00:28,062
Start on Synthesis.

6
00:00:28,162 --> 00:00:33,777
Choose the technology, the response type,
the topology, and the frequency.

7
00:00:33,877 --> 00:00:41,887
Here, a microstrip low-pass at 1
gigahertz, pi to pi, four sections, with 50
ohms at both ends.

8
00:00:41,987 --> 00:00:45,208
The calculator builds the ladder instantly.

9
00:00:45,308 --> 00:00:48,142
It starts from the prototype elements:

10
00:00:48,242 --> 00:00:53,317
Cp is 1 over omega-c Z-zero, and Lp is
Z-zero over omega-c.

11
00:00:53,417 --> 00:00:59,881
For a plain pi ladder, Z-zero is 0.8
times source resistance, 40 ohms.

12
00:00:59,981 --> 00:01:08,917
That gives 3.98 picofarads for end
capacitors, and 12.73 nanohenries for
series inductors, twice Lp.

13
00:01:09,017 --> 00:01:12,315
The interior shunt capacitor is doubled too:

14
00:01:12,415 --> 00:01:13,551
at 7.96 picofarads.

15
00:01:13,651 --> 00:01:14,632
Five elements:

16
00:01:14,732 --> 00:01:17,335
three capacitors and two inductors.

17
00:01:17,435 --> 00:01:23,900
You can also pick T topologies, high-pass or
band-pass responses, and steepened ends.

18
00:01:26,000 --> 00:01:28,356
Now the microstrip realization.

19
00:01:28,456 --> 00:01:35,884
Inductors become narrow, high-impedance
lines, and capacitors become wide, low-
impedance lines.

20
00:01:35,984 --> 00:01:45,075
On one-millimeter RO4003C, a 120-ohm line is
just 0.34 millimeters wide, and a 20-ohm
line is 7.86 millimeters wide.

21
00:01:45,175 --> 00:01:51,414
The widths come from the Hammerstad and
Jensen model for effective permittivity.

22
00:01:51,514 --> 00:01:56,881
Lengths follow from the element values, with
first-order corrections.

23
00:01:56,981 --> 00:02:04,725
The fifty-ohm feed lines are 2.25
millimeters wide, and the whole layout is
137.8 millimeters long.

24
00:02:04,825 --> 00:02:07,815
The calculator also checks feasibility.

25
00:02:07,915 --> 00:02:19,384
Here the middle capacitor section is 36
millimeters, longer than a wavelength over
eight, so it warns that the lumped
approximation is poor there.

26
00:02:19,484 --> 00:02:23,900
You can also shorten open ends by the
fringing extension.

27
00:02:26,000 --> 00:02:30,568
The Frequency Response page uses an exact
network analysis.

28
00:02:30,668 --> 00:02:41,646
Every element becomes a two-by-two ABCD
matrix, the matrices are multiplied in
signal order, and S21 and S11 come straight
from the product.

29
00:02:41,746 --> 00:02:44,970
The blue curve is the ideal lumped ladder:

30
00:02:45,070 --> 00:02:54,465
minus three decibels at 0.95 gigahertz, 5.6
decibels down at the nominal cutoff, and
minus 42 decibels at two gigahertz.

31
00:02:54,565 --> 00:02:56,997
Dashed curve shows return loss.

32
00:02:57,097 --> 00:03:05,384
Now switch on the microstrip overlay, which
uses ideal transmission-line sections
instead of lumped parts.

33
00:03:05,484 --> 00:03:16,225
The edge moves down to 0.74 gigahertz, the
best rejection is about minus 29 decibels,
and a spurious passband appears near 2.3
gigahertz.

34
00:03:16,325 --> 00:03:23,900
That is the price of stepped-impedance
lines, and the overlay shows it before you
build anything.

35
00:03:26,000 --> 00:03:27,749
Need a sharper skirt?

36
00:03:27,849 --> 00:03:36,905
Steepening replaces the end elements with LC
tanks that place an attenuation pole just
above the cutoff.

37
00:03:37,005 --> 00:03:45,885
Set the pole at 1.3 gigahertz, so the ratio
is 1.3, and the derivation parameter m comes
out at 0.639.

38
00:03:45,985 --> 00:03:55,569
The end elements become 2 picofarads in
series with 7.49 nanohenries, and the
interior values change slightly.

39
00:03:55,669 --> 00:03:57,242
Compare the curves:

40
00:03:57,342 --> 00:04:04,550
at 1.2 gigahertz the plain filter is 16
decibels down, and the steepened one is 35.

41
00:04:04,650 --> 00:04:07,367
The loss at cutoff improves too.

42
00:04:07,467 --> 00:04:13,882
The same idea works for high-pass filters,
with the pole below the cutoff.

43
00:04:13,982 --> 00:04:18,900
The price is a stopband that rises again
beyond the pole.

44
00:04:21,000 --> 00:04:29,350
For band-pass filters, the calculator
designs parallel-coupled microstrip lines,
using Cohn's method.

45
00:04:29,450 --> 00:04:35,876
Here the band is 900 to 1100 megahertz, a
twenty percent fractional bandwidth.

46
00:04:35,976 --> 00:04:50,267
Each coupled section is a quarter
wavelength, 44.9 millimeters, and the layout
is the classic edge-coupled staircase, each
resonator offset by one strip width plus one
gap.

47
00:04:50,367 --> 00:04:56,960
The even- and odd-mode impedances come from
the prototype coupling coefficients:

48
00:04:57,060 --> 00:05:02,733
67.8 and 30 ohms for end sections, 50.9
and 33.1 in the middle.

49
00:05:02,833 --> 00:05:06,414
That sets gaps of 0.48 and 0.93 millimeters.

50
00:05:06,514 --> 00:05:13,776
The response shows the passband between the
band edges, with steep skirts on both sides.

51
00:05:13,876 --> 00:05:23,900
The default band, 800 to 1200 megahertz, is
flagged, because forty-one percent bandwidth
is too wide for these equations.

52
00:05:26,000 --> 00:05:31,275
The Wave Impedance page explains how the
termination is chosen.

53
00:05:31,375 --> 00:05:44,926
The image impedance of a T section falls to
zero at cutoff, a pi section rises toward
infinity, and the m-derived section stays
almost flat across the passband.

54
00:05:45,026 --> 00:05:51,666
The program terminates the filter with the
image impedance at omega equals 0.6.

55
00:05:51,766 --> 00:05:58,833
A plain pi ladder gives Z-zero of 40
ohms, matching source to load.

56
00:05:58,933 --> 00:06:04,719
With a pi input and a T output, the filter
also transforms impedance:

57
00:06:04,819 --> 00:06:07,023
50 ohms in, 32 ohms out.

58
00:06:07,123 --> 00:06:08,900
T to pi yields 78 ohms.

59
00:06:11,000 --> 00:06:18,984
The Table page lists every element with its
value, strip width, and length, ready for
the board.

60
00:06:19,084 --> 00:06:22,858
Below it are the automatic feasibility
checks:

61
00:06:22,958 --> 00:06:36,416
sections longer than a wavelength over
eight, lines outside the etchable range,
coupled-line bandwidths above thirty
percent, and parts too small to be reliable.

62
00:06:36,516 --> 00:06:37,847
And the exports:

63
00:06:37,947 --> 00:06:51,995
a DXF layout in millimeters, a KiCad
footprint, a SPICE netlist, a Touchstone S2P
file, a CSV table, a PNG image, and a design
file or link that restores every setting.

64
00:06:52,095 --> 00:06:58,900
So you can go from an idea to a
manufacturable layout without leaving the
browser.

65
00:07:01,000 --> 00:07:04,749
The theory page documents every formula:

66
00:07:04,849 --> 00:07:17,257
symbols, prototype synthesis, terminations,
the band-pass equations, the microstrip
model, network analysis, and a worked
example.

67
00:07:17,357 --> 00:07:28,900
The references run from Zobel and Richards
to Cohn, Matthaei, Young and Jones, Wheeler,
Hammerstad and Jensen, and Pozar.

68
00:07:31,000 --> 00:07:34,083
From specification to layout:

69
00:07:34,183 --> 00:07:40,888
LC values, microstrip dimensions, exact
response, and exports.

70
00:07:40,988 --> 00:07:48,900
Pick a filter, change a number, and watch
the layout and response follow.
